// Jahshaka's DDGI diffuse INTENSITY — the one thing upstream's IrradianceField
// has no knob for (GI_UNIFIED_SPEC.md §4 P1 / addendum item 2 — that is the
// earlier spec of the program now called PHOTON; SPECS/PHOTON_SPEC.md is live).
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Binding an IrradianceField makes HlmsPbs set
// `VctDisableDiffuse` (OgreHlmsPbs.cpp:1846-1850): DDGI does not ADD to voxel
// cone tracing's diffuse, it REPLACES it — and the P0 spike measured the
// replacement at ~13x dimmer than the (over-bright, leak-prone) VCT diffuse it
// takes over from. Every scene therefore gets markedly darker the moment the
// field binds, and `IrradianceFieldSettings` carries nothing to scale it with.
// So the scalar is OURS, applied at the last line of upstream's own piece, and
// it rides the pass buffer (a const-buffer write, never a shader rebuild) so
// the author can turn the dial without a recompile.
// WHY FIVE HOOK PIECES AND NOT A COPY OF UPSTREAM'S. This file used to
// UNDEFINE upstream's `applyIrradianceField` and define it again with the whole
// body copied verbatim, because the terms below are not a post-multiply: the
// sample point moves before the cage is derived, the cage's probe indices have
// to be clamped, two quantities are accumulated per probe, and the field's
// answer is scaled and then complemented. There was no hook for any of it, and
// a duplicated body is a liability exactly once -- at a pin bump, silently --
// so it was guarded by a configure-time sha256 of upstream's file that FAILED
// the build when upstream moved.
//
// Ogre-patch 0058 puts the five insertion points into upstream's own piece
// (prologue / probe-origin / per-probe / resolve / post), which is what this
// file uses now. The copy and the hash tripwire are gone with it: ours is a set
// of PIECES again, upstream's body is upstream's, and a pin bump either applies
// the patch or refuses it out loud. The patch also carries the two fixes the
// copy had folded into the body -- the division guard on a probe that lands
// exactly under the shaded pixel, and the comment-promised clamp becoming a
// hook because the per-axis probe counts a clamp needs are not in upstream's
// render params.
//
// CAUTION, the same one JahFog and JahVct carry, and this file cost the lane a
// full test run by ignoring it: the Hlms parser does NOT respect // comments —
// an at-directive written inside a comment is EXECUTED, and a stray parenthesis
// after one is a syntax error that silently drops every definition in the file
// (the shader still compiles; it just uses upstream's piece, so the symptom is
// "my override does nothing"). Every directive is therefore named in WORDS
// above, never written with its at-sign.
//
// THE SECOND THING THAT IS NOT UPSTREAM'S: THE AMBIENT SKY-VISIBILITY TERM
// (the Photon ambient fix; GI_UNIFIED_SPEC.md ADDENDUM CORRECTION, and the
// ledger entries "Vct_piece_ps.any: volume-local ambient blend commented out"
// and "IrradianceField nits" in SPECS/OGRE_UPSTREAM_ISSUES.md).
//
// THE GAP. Inside a VCT volume the PBS ambient term is dead: every ambient
// piece is wrapped in `if( vctSpecular.w == 0 )` and upstream commented the
// volume-local blend out, hardcoding blend = 1.0, so vctSpecular.w is never
// zero and DoAmbientLighting can never fire while VctLighting is bound. The
// only live ambient in a VCT scene is the cone-traced diffuse's own
// `light.xyz += ambient * light.w` — ambient times the ESCAPE FRACTION of its
// cones, i.e. ambient weighted by how much sky the surface can see. Binding an
// irradiance field sets VctDisableDiffuse, which deletes that whole branch, so
// with DDGI on the ambient inside the volume has to come from the field.
//
// HOW MUCH SKY THIS POINT SEES — THE ONE DEFINITION (PHOTON-READER-1). It is the
// ESCAPE FRACTION of the voxel march, `1 - min( 1, opacity / 0.95 )`: the share
// of a cone or ray the voxels did not stop. Both sides march through the one
// voxel radiance reader (jah_voxel_march.glsl / jah_voxel_sample.glsl — the
// pixel's cones, the bounce job and the field's generation job). The pixel's
// cones weight their six escapes into `light.w`; the field's generation job
// stores each probe ray's escape in the DEPTH atlas's third channel, the
// integration job convolves it over the same cosine lobe as the depth moments,
// and this file reads it at the pixel's normal from every probe of the cage,
// weighted exactly as the irradiance is. So `ifdSkyVis` is the cage's
// trilinear, visibility-weighted reading of the escape its probes' rays
// actually measured, integrated by 144 cosine-distributed rays per probe
// instead of six cones per pixel. The OPACITY differs by what starts where: a
// cone that starts ON a surface uses the march's occupancy estimate (it would
// otherwise occlude itself in its own cell), a probe ray starts in free space
// and uses its own composite, which for a ray crossing a wall is the
// transmittance (IrradianceFieldGenFromVct_piece_cs.any says why, measured).
// There is no model in it any more: the Chebyshev proxy that reconstructed an
// escape from the depth moments (a two-point mixture at a fitted escape
// distance, a 0.9 scale, a 0.3 knee) is deleted with the march that threw the
// real escape away. A sealed room's rays all stop in its walls, so its interior
// probes store exactly zero.
//
// The ambient itself is the scene's SH irradiance at the pixel's normal, gated
// on `ambient_sh` — the only ambient mode this engine ever sets
// (OgreEngine::ensureHlms forces HlmsPbs::AmbientSh). The MONOCHROME SH variant
// is excluded: its irradianceSH returns a scalar, so the term would not compile.
// (That the cones' ambient is the 2-band hemisphere pair and this one the full
// SH is the environment's question — one sky term for every escape — not the
// escape's.)
//
// THE DIAL is `GiParams::ddgiAmbient`, riding jahIfd.y beside the intensity, and
// 0 turns the whole term off through a UNIFORM branch — so "DDGI without the
// sky term" is not a rebuild, it is a const-buffer write, which is what lets the
// suite A/B it and what makes the sealed-room invariance assertion meaningful.
// jahIfd.zw carry the field's Y and Z probe counts, which upstream's own render
// params do NOT contain (only Nx and Nx*Ny reach the shader,
// OgreIrradianceField.cpp:812-813); the cage clamp below needs all three.
//
// THE THIRD THING THAT IS NOT UPSTREAM'S: THE FIELD'S EDGE
// (ENGINE_CACHE_POLICY_SPEC.md P9, re-scoped 2026-09-13; the owner's report was
// "the Grand Showroom 2's roof shows texture maps", and the diagnosis is
// spikes/showroom2-roof/ + spikes/e3-ifdedge/).
//
// THE DEFECT IS UPSTREAM'S AND IT IS TWO DEFECTS. (1) The cage loop's comment
// says "clamp to the probe grid boundary" and no clamp is performed, here or in
// the world->grid map (OgreIrradianceField.cpp:794-812), so a pixel outside the
// grid indexes past it and getProbeIdx's FLAT index wraps into another probe
// row -- a surface above a building is handed the irradiance of a probe inside
// it. (2) Even with the cage clamped the field answers everywhere, because the
// sum is NORMALISED by sumIfdWeight: a pixel a hundred metres outside the
// volume still gets the border probes' irradiance at full strength, since
// crushing all eight weights only renormalises the same eight probes. The
// Chebyshev visibility test cannot rescue either case, and that was MEASURED
// rather than assumed (the note in the loop).
//
// WHAT THIS FILE DOES ABOUT IT, in three parts, none of which costs a sample:
//   * the CLAMP the comment promises;
//   * a CONFIDENCE in [0, 1] that is 1 wherever the field has something to say
//     and 0 where it does not -- zero outside the AUTHORED volume (the box the
//     host handed initialize(), recoverable from the probe counts alone), and
//     zero on a surface with no probe on its own side;
//   * a FALLBACK, so that "the field says nothing" means "what this pixel had
//     before a field was ever bound" rather than "black": the voxel-cone
//     ambient term that binding a field deletes.
// Inside the field the confidence is exactly 1 and every frame is what it was:
// gi.modes, gi.ddgi, gi.ddgi_ambient and gi.ddgi_raster print identical numbers
// with this file and without it.
//
// SCOPE: the whole file is gated on the `irradiance_field` property, which
// HlmsPbs sets only while a field is bound. With no field bound (every scene
// that has not opted in, which in P1 is all of them by default) not one line of
// this file reaches the generated shader, and undefining a piece that was never
// defined is a no-op.

@property( irradiance_field )

// ---- THE SAMPLE POINT, AND EVERYTHING THE CAGE LOOP BELOW NEEDS --------
//
// Inserted by upstream's piece with ifdGridPos and ifdSpaceNormal in scope and
// before the cage is derived from them (ogre-patch 0058).
// THE FIELD STANDS DOWN WHERE A SCREEN PROBE ANSWERED THE PIXEL
// (GATHER-1a, ogre-patch 0086). THE ACCOUNTING RULE OF THIS FILE, in its third
// and last half: a pixel receives exactly ONE diffuse-GI term. The voxel-cone
// diffuse is compiled out by a pass property under the gather; the gather's own
// term is added at upstream's irradiance-volumes insertion point; and the cage
// below is declined here, for the pixels a probe covered and for no others. The
// patch's hook is the only way to decline it -- every other insertion point in
// this piece is INSIDE the body, and subtracting the field's answer back out
// after paying for eight irradiance fetches, eight depth fetches and a
// Chebyshev test is not the same thing.
//
// `w` OF THE GATHER'S TEXTURE IS ITS COVERAGE: one where a probe answered for
// this pixel, zero where none did (rq_probe_integrate.comp). At phase 1 it is
// exactly binary, so this is a switch; when phase 2's plane-weighted integrate
// makes it a fraction, the field's contribution wants to be SCALED by the
// complement rather than switched, which is a change to the patch's condition
// and to this line together.
//
// The guard is the SIX TERMS the texture's own declaration carries, word for
// word (JahFog_piece_vs_piece_ps.any): a piece that reads a texture declared
// under a different condition is a compile error that loses the frame.
@property( jah_probe_gather && hlms_screen_pos_int && needs_env_brdf && !hlms_prepass && !hlms_shadowcaster && !irradiance_volumes )
@piece( custom_ps_ifdCageGate )
	ifdCageEnabled = 1.0 - saturate( OGRE_Load2D( jahProbeIrradiance, iFragCoord, 0 ).w );
@end
@end

@piece( custom_ps_ifdCagePrologue )
	// THE FOURTH THING THAT IS NOT UPSTREAM'S: THE SURFACE BIAS (ENGINE-6 item
	// 1; PHOTON-S2 FINDINGS §5 item 2 and §7 item 1, recorded in
	// SPECS/OGRE_UPSTREAM_ISSUES.md as "IrradianceField: three visibility
	// findings").
	//
	// THE DEFECT, upstream's: the cage is looked up AT THE SHADED POINT. A
	// point lying ON a surface is, to a probe on the other side of that
	// surface, at very nearly the distance the depth atlas recorded for the
	// surface itself -- so the Chebyshev test is evaluated on its own knife
	// edge, where the stored variance decides the answer. With
	// `mNumRaysPerPixel = 1` (our setting, and upstream's own default in the
	// sample) the depth moments come from ONE ray per texel convolved by the
	// integration job, so that variance is small and the test is very nearly a
	// hard step: a wall's two faces are a texel apart in it. That is the
	// standard DDGI leak pair, and the paper's own answer is not a better test
	// but a MOVED SAMPLE POINT -- shift the lookup off the surface, along the
	// normal and towards the eye, by a fraction of a probe spacing. The wall's
	// far side then sits a real distance behind the sample and the Chebyshev
	// test has something to bite on.
	//
	// THE UNITS ARE PROBE SPACINGS, which is why this is applied HERE, in grid
	// space, and not in metres in view space: one unit of ifdGridPos is one
	// probe spacing ON THAT AXIS, so an anisotropic field (32x8x32 is what our
	// fit gives a room) biases by more metres along its sparse axis, which is
	// exactly where its depth texels are coarsest. A metre-valued bias would
	// need the spacing to convert and would be wrong on two axes out of three.
	//
	// THE TWO CONSTANTS are the DDGI paper's own MIXTURE -- 0.2 along the
	// normal to 0.8 towards the eye (Majercik et al. 2019 §4.2; RTXGI ships
	// the same shape as probeNormalBias/probeViewBias) -- at a QUARTER of the
	// paper's magnitude: it writes `( 0.2 n + 0.8 w_o ) * 0.75` spacings,
	// this is the same vector times 0.25. The view half dominates because a
	// grazing view is the case that leaks: the shading point is then nearly IN
	// the surface as the probe sees it, whatever the normal does.
	//
	// WHY A QUARTER, measured in this lane on the PHOTON-S2 leak room
	// (0.5/0.2/0.1/0.05 m walls, one lamp outside) against gi.ddgi_edge's
	// legitimate red bounce and the brightness of a floor 5 m from any wall.
	// The bias trades one against the other MONOTONICALLY, because lifting a
	// probe's false self-occlusion and lifting its true occlusion are the same
	// operation:
	//
	//    view bias | 0.10 m wall leak | 0.05 m wall leak | open floor
	//       0.00   |      0.0520      |      0.0807      |   +3/255
	//       0.20   |      0.0436      |      0.0694      |  +13/255
	//       0.60   |      0.0415      |      0.0641      |  +22/255
	//     (today)  |      0.0536      |      0.0829      |     --
	//
	// The knee is at 0.20: it takes 87 % of the leak the full paper value
	// takes, for little more than half of the interior brightening -- and that
	// brightening is a change to every shipped DDGI picture, not a test
	// number. The normal half is scaled with it to keep the paper's ratio.
	const float ifdNormalBias = 0.05;   // probe spacings
	const float ifdViewBias   = 0.20;   // probe spacings
	// The direction TOWARDS the eye: inPs.pos is a VIEW-space position, so the
	// eye is the origin. Normalised in view space and then rotated into grid
	// space (transformDir3 is the rotation half of the same transform), which
	// makes it a direction in probe spacings -- deliberately NOT re-normalised
	// afterwards, so an anisotropic grid biases per axis as described above.
	float3 ifdViewDirGrid = transformDir3( normalize( -inPs.pos.xyz ), //
										   passBuf.irradianceField.viewToIrradianceFieldRow0,
										   passBuf.irradianceField.viewToIrradianceFieldRow1,
										   passBuf.irradianceField.viewToIrradianceFieldRow2 );
	ifdGridPos += ifdSpaceNormal * ifdNormalBias + ifdViewDirGrid * ifdViewBias;

	// OURS, AND THE THIRD THING THAT IS NOT UPSTREAM'S: THE FIELD'S EDGE
	// (ENGINE_CACHE_POLICY_SPEC P9). Two numbers the cage loop below needs and
	// upstream never computes.
	//
	//   ifdMaxProbe  the last legal probe index on each axis. Upstream's own
	//                render params carry only Nx and Nx*Ny, so Y and Z ride
	//                jahIfd.zw. When they are absent -- the host pushed no
	//                IFD state, which cannot happen today because the arm writes
	//                the state before it binds -- the bound is set beyond any
	//                real grid, and every line below reduces to upstream's
	//                behaviour exactly.
	//   (the fade that uses them is after the loop, with its derivation)
	float3 ifdProbeCounts = float3( passBuf.irradianceField.numProbesAggregated.x,
									passBuf.jahIfd.z, passBuf.jahIfd.w );
	bool  ifdHaveCounts = passBuf.jahIfd.z > 0.5;
	float3 ifdMaxProbe = ifdHaveCounts ? ifdProbeCounts - float3( 1.0, 1.0, 1.0 )
									   : float3( 1e9, 1e9, 1e9 );
	// How much of the cage's final weight comes from probes IN FRONT of the
	// shading surface (P9; the fade after the loop says what it is for).
	float ifdFrontWeight = 0;
@property( ambient_sh && !ambient_sh_monochrome && vct_num_probes )
	// OURS: the cage-weighted sky visibility (the header: the stored escape of
	// the probes' rays, read per cage probe below).
	float ifdSkyVis = 0;
@end
@end

// ---- THE CAGE'S PROBE INDICES, CLAMPED TO THE GRID ---------------------
@piece( custom_ps_ifdCageProbeOrigin )
		// THE CLAMP UPSTREAM'S COMMENT PROMISES AND UPSTREAM CANNOT PERFORM: the
		// per-axis probe counts it needs are not in the render params (only Nx and
		// Nx*Ny are), which is why ogre-patch 0058 makes this a hook rather than a
		// fix in place. The world->grid map has no clamp either
		// (OgreIrradianceField.cpp:794-812). Without it a pixel outside the
		// field indexes past the grid and getProbeIdx's flat index WRAPS into
		// another row -- in the Grand Showroom 2 the roof's upper cage corner
		// landed on the FLOOR layer with 77% of the trilinear weight, which is
		// the "texture maps on the roof" the owner reported. A clamped cage
		// EXTRAPOLATES the border probes instead, which is bounded and which
		// the two fades in the resolve piece then take to zero.
		probeOrigin = clamp( probeOrigin, float3( 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 ), ifdMaxProbe );
@end

// ---- WHAT WE ACCUMULATE PER CAGE PROBE ---------------------------------
@piece( custom_ps_ifdCageProbe )
		// A SECOND READING OF UPSTREAM'S OWN FRONT/BACK TEST, for the front-side
		// share of the cage's weight (P9; the fade that uses it is in the resolve
		// piece). The +0.5 is what separates
		// BEHIND from COPLANAR: a probe exactly in the shading surface -- which
		// is the normal case for a flat field, e.g. the default scene's 64x2x64
		// field puts a probe layer exactly in the ground plane -- has dot 0 and
		// is not behind anything, so it reads 0.5 and keeps its say, while a
		// probe genuinely under the surface reads 0 (the x200 makes even a
		// 1 cm offset saturate).
		// Recomputed here rather than carried out of upstream's weight line, which
		// is upstream's and stays upstream's: the same expression on the same
		// normalised `dir` the weight was built from, so the same value.
		float ifdFrontSide = saturate( dot( dir, ifdSpaceNormal ) * 200.0 + 0.5 );
		ifdFrontWeight += weight * ifdFrontSide;

		// WHY THERE IS NO VISIBILITY TERM OF OURS HERE, and it was measured
		// rather than assumed (the lead's question, P9). The Chebyshev weight
		// cannot carry the edge case on its own for two reasons. First,
		// arithmetic: the sum is divided by sumIfdWeight below, so crushing ALL
		// eight weights does not remove the contribution -- it renormalises the
		// same eight probes and the leak survives at full strength. Second, and
		// the reason a cage-visibility fraction was tried and then deleted: in
		// the suite's sealed box the trilinear-weighted Chebyshev fraction of a
		// roof pixel whose ENTIRE cage is embedded in the ceiling slab measures
		// above 0.999 -- the depth atlas reports those probes as unoccluded, so
		// no threshold on it can separate them from an interior floor. The
		// probes that answer such a pixel are inside geometry; recognising them
		// is probe classification (RTXGI-style), which this lane does not do.

@property( ambient_sh && !ambient_sh_monochrome && vct_num_probes )
		// OURS: this cage probe's stored escape at the pixel's normal (the depth
		// atlas's third channel — the header), weighted exactly as its irradiance
		// is. The branch is UNIFORM (jahIfd.y is a pass constant), so with the dial
		// at 0 not one instruction of this executes.
		if( passBuf.jahIfd.y > 0.0 )
		{
			float2 ifdVisUV = getIrradianceFieldXY(
				ifdSpaceNormal, probeIdx, passBuf.irradianceField.depthBorderedRes,
				passBuf.irradianceField.depthFullWidth,
				passBuf.irradianceField.depthInvFullResolution );
			ifdSkyVis += weight * OGRE_SampleLevel( ifdDepth, ifdSampler, ifdVisUV, 0 ).z;
		}
@end
@end

// ---- HOW MUCH THE FIELD IS TRUSTED, AND THE SCALE IT IS GIVEN ----------
@piece( custom_ps_ifdCageResolve )
	// OURS (P9): HOW MUCH THE FIELD IS TRUSTED AT THIS PIXEL, in [0, 1], and
	// the two independent ways it can have nothing to say.
	//
	// (a) OUTSIDE THE AUTHORED VOLUME.
	//
	// THE AUTHORED VOLUME, IN GRID SPACE, WITH NO NEW UNIFORM. `initialize()`
	// enlarges the volume it was given by one probe block per side before it
	// builds the world->grid transform (OgreIrradianceField.cpp:543-547):
	//     O' = O - S/N,   S' = S + 2S/N
	// and the transform maps [O', O'+S'] onto [0, N]
	// (OgreIrradianceField.cpp:794-800). So the AUTHORED corner O lands at
	//     ( O - O' ) * N / S' = ( S/N ) * N / ( S ( N+2 ) / N ) = N / ( N+2 )
	// and the authored box is exactly [ q, N - q ] with q = N / (N+2), per axis,
	// computable from the probe counts alone. (Checked against a live field:
	// N_y = 16 over bounds y [-1, 5.25] gives q = 0.889 and the bounds' own
	// corners measure at grid 0.889 and 15.111.)
	//
	// OUTSIDE IT THE FIELD SAYS NOTHING. Not "says something weaker": the
	// volume is what was voxelised and what the probes integrate, and a surface
	// far beyond it has no probe on its own side at all -- the cage is clamped,
	// so it is being handed the border probes' irradiance, which is the leak
	// this fix exists for.
	//
	// WHERE THE ROLL-OFF SITS, AND WHY IT MOVED OUTWARD (SQUARE-1, 2026-09-17,
	// measured; the owner's report was "a square lighting effect around the
	// scene in an empty scene, not centred on the scene").
	//
	// The roll-off used to be half a probe spacing wide and to live INSIDE the
	// authored box, so the field reached zero AT the face. That is the wrong
	// side, and the reason is what the field hands over TO. Under a cascade
	// chain the field rides cascade 0, so the face is also the chain's own hop,
	// and the cone term is NOT continuous across it: measured on the default
	// scene (flat floor, straight down from 4 m, Epic, plain grade) the cones
	// alone read 110/255 outside cascade 0 and 98-100 inside it, an 11/255 step
	// at the face that has nothing to do with the field. With the ramp inside
	// the box, every pixel in that last half spacing was handed the INSIDE
	// cone value while the field was still fading up -- so the profile fell
	// off the outside value (110) to the inside cone value (101), then climbed
	// back to the field's 107 over half a spacing. A DARK LINE along the face,
	// one probe spacing wide, 7-9/255 deep on the coarse axes: the sharpest
	// feature of the square and the one an eye finds.
	//
	// So the ramp now sits OUTSIDE the authored face, over the padding shell
	// `initialize()` adds: it enlarges the volume by one probe block per side
	// before building the world->grid transform, so on the LOW faces the
	// lattice reaches ~0.89-0.97 grid units beyond the authored corner and a
	// cage taken half a unit outside it is made of real, traced probes with no
	// clamp active at all. Upstream's padding is ASYMMETRIC (the same asymmetry
	// the front-weight fade below records): probe N-1 sits ~0.11 units INSIDE
	// the high authored face, so on that side the outward ramp rides the
	// clamped border probes -- an extrapolation, but a bounded one that the
	// ramp is already fading out, and measured below to be monotone.
	//
	// The field is therefore at FULL confidence at the face and fades to
	// nothing half a probe spacing outside it, and the hand-off to the cone
	// term is a RAMP in one direction instead of a notch: measured
	// 107 -> 110 monotonically over that half spacing on both the low and the
	// high face, with the dark line gone.
	//
	// IT HIDES THE EDGE; THE ONE READER EQUALISES THE INTEGRALS. The field used
	// to integrate cascade 0 alone and throw the escape away while the cones
	// walked the whole chain and took the sky as their escape (4-6/255 apart on
	// an open floor whatever the ramp did). Since PHOTON-READER-1 both march the
	// same chain through the same reader and both weight the sky by the same
	// escape, so what is left between them is the integration (144 rays per
	// probe against six cones per pixel) and the environment term.
	float3 ifdQ = ifdProbeCounts / ( ifdProbeCounts + float3( 2.0, 2.0, 2.0 ) );
	float3 ifdInside = min( ifdGridPos - ifdQ, ( ifdProbeCounts - ifdQ ) - ifdGridPos );
	// `ifdInside` is the signed distance to the nearest authored face in PROBE
	// SPACINGS, positive inside. 1 at the face, 0 one ramp width outside it.
	const float ifdEdgeRamp = 0.5;			// probe spacings, outside the face
	float3 ifdAxisFade = saturate( ifdInside * ( 1.0 / ifdEdgeRamp ) + 1.0 );
	float ifdEdgeFade = ifdAxisFade.x * ifdAxisFade.y * ifdAxisFade.z;
	ifdEdgeFade = ifdEdgeFade * ifdEdgeFade * ( 3.0 - 2.0 * ifdEdgeFade );
	// (b) NO PROBE IN FRONT OF ME. The fade above is about the field's BOX; this
	// one is about the last probe LAYER, and it is what the owner's report
	// needed. Ogre places probe i at fieldOrigin + i * fieldSize / N over a box
	// that was padded symmetrically, so the lattice reaches one probe block
	// BELOW the authored volume and stops a fraction of a block INSIDE its top
	// (upstream asymmetry; in the Grand Showroom 2 the topmost probe layer sits
	// at y 7.731, which is 2 cm under the roof's outer face and INSIDE the 0.5 m
	// ceiling slab). A pixel on that roof therefore has a perfectly legal cage
	// in which EVERY probe is behind the surface and inside the slab, and the
	// field hands it the inside of the building. `ifdFrontWeight` is the share
	// of the normalised weight that comes from probes on the surface's own
	// side; when it is zero there is no probe lighting this surface at all and
	// the field has nothing to say. It is exactly zero for the roof case and
	// well clear of the knee everywhere else, because upstream's own weights
	// already favour a front probe 6:1 (1.2 against the 0.2 floor) and a
	// coplanar probe counts as half -- an interior floor a centimetre above a
	// probe plane measures 0.06, and one lying exactly ON a probe layer 0.5.
	// This is NOT probe classification: it does not detect a probe inside
	// geometry, it detects a SURFACE with no probe in front of it.
	const float ifdFrontLo = 0.005;
	const float ifdFrontHi = 0.05;
	// THE DIVISOR CAN BE ZERO, and the guard has to be SCALE-FREE or it changes a
	// shipped picture (the lead's read of GATHER-1a). This ratio is a SHARE --
	// how much of the cage's weight comes from probes in front of the surface --
	// and both terms carry the same crushed scale: a fully crushed cage sums to
	// about 1e-17 (upstream's own 1e-6 weight floor, cubed by the trilinear
	// product and divided by the 0.04 crush threshold), and its front share is
	// crushed by exactly as much, so the RATIO is still 1 and the field fades to
	// nothing deliberately. Flooring the DENOMINATOR at 1e-6 instead makes that
	// ratio 1e-11, the confidence 0 and the fallback 1 -- the full cone diffuse
	// or a flat ambient, leaking in exactly where this term exists to stop it,
	// at every tier, with the gather nowhere in the frame. So: a zero divisor is
	// "no front share to compute", which is a share of ONE (the cage said
	// nothing; the clamp above it is what decides), and any non-zero divisor is
	// used as it stands however small it is.
	float ifdFrontFade = smoothstep( ifdFrontLo, ifdFrontHi,
									 sumIfdWeight > 0.0 ? ifdFrontWeight / sumIfdWeight : 1.0 );
	// The counts are absent only in a state the host cannot produce (the GI arm
	// writes them before it binds the field); there, the box fade is skipped.
	float ifdConfidence = min( ifdHaveCounts ? ifdEdgeFade : 1.0, ifdFrontFade );

	// (c) OURS, AND THE SECOND HALF OF ENGINE-6 ITEM 1: NO PROBE OF THIS CAGE
	// CAN SEE ME.
	//
	// THE DEFECT, upstream's, and the one the cage loop's own note above
	// records as arithmetic: the sum below is NORMALISED by `sumIfdWeight`, so
	// crushing every probe of a cage does not remove its contribution -- it
	// renormalises the same eight crushed probes and hands the pixel the full
	// leak back. The Chebyshev test can say "none of these probes can see
	// you"; the division then throws that answer away. (PHOTON-S2 measured the
	// consequence from the other side: a distance field that crushes MORE
	// sharply bought 4-24 % of the leak, because the renormalisation gives it
	// back.)
	//
	// WHAT THE NUMBER MEANS. `weight` starts at `saturate( dot ) + 0.2`, so a
	// probe that is merely behind the surface still carries 0.2, and the
	// trilinear factors sum to 1 over the cage: an UNCRUSHED cage therefore
	// sums to at least 0.2 and a well-lit one to about 1.2. The crush cubes
	// anything under 0.2, and a fully crushed cage lands at ~1e-17. There is
	// no legitimate cage anywhere in between, which is why the fade can sit
	// four orders of magnitude BELOW the uncrushed floor and still separate
	// the two cases perfectly: it is a guard against a division, not a dial on
	// the picture. The measured cost of it on gi.ddgi_edge's legitimate red
	// bounce is zero (lane report).
	//
	// WHAT IT FADES TO: nothing, deliberately -- not to the VCT ambient
	// fallback below. `ifdConfidence` means "this pixel is outside the field's
	// reach", and there the ambient a no-field build gave the pixel is the
	// right answer. THIS says "the field can see this pixel and reports that
	// nothing lights it from here", which is a real answer; substituting an
	// unoccluded ambient for it would re-open the leak in a different colour.
	const float ifdWeightLo = 0.0005;
	const float ifdWeightHi = 0.0050;
	float ifdWeightFade = smoothstep( ifdWeightLo, ifdWeightHi, sumIfdWeight );

@property( jah_probe_gather && hlms_screen_pos_int && needs_env_brdf && !hlms_prepass && !hlms_shadowcaster && !irradiance_volumes )
	// ...AND WHERE THE CAGE WAS DECLINED, THE FIELD OWES NOTHING EITHER. Both
	// terms of the post hook below are the field's own fallbacks -- what a pixel
	// gets INSTEAD of the cage -- and a pixel a screen probe answered is not
	// owed either of them. Saying it as "full confidence, no sky visibility"
	// rather than with a third branch keeps the post hook's arithmetic exactly
	// what it is for every other pixel.
	if( ifdCageEnabled == 0.0 )
	{
		ifdConfidence = 1.0;
		ifdSkyVis = 0.0;
	}
@end

	// THE SCALE UPSTREAM'S COMPOSITE LINE APPLIES (ogre-patch 0058). Upstream
	// writes
	//     pixelData.envColourD.xyz += ifdIrradiance * ( ifdIrradianceScale / sumIfdWeight );
	// with the scale at one, and we set it to jahIfd.x -- the DDGI diffuse
	// intensity the host writes into the pass buffer (JahshakaEngine's
	// FogHlmsListener; GiParams::ddgiIntensity, 1 = the field's own answer) --
	// times the confidence and the weight fade above.
	// At intensity 1 inside the field every factor is exactly 1 and the line is
	// bit-for-bit what it always was.
	//
	// ...TIMES THE VOXEL VOLUME'S OWN DECODE MULTIPLIER (PHOTON-M2, F-C), which
	// is the unit that was missing from this line.
	//
	// The field's atlas holds the mean of RAW LIGHT-VOXEL SAMPLES: the Gen job
	// cone-traces the voxel volume and stores the average with no scale of its
	// own (IrradianceFieldGenFromVct_piece_cs.any's BodyCS: `diffuse.xyz *=
	// invNumRaysPerIrradiancePixel`, straight into a R10G10B10A2_UNORM atlas).
	// Those samples are NORMALISED: the light injection scales everything by
	// `bakingMultiplier = PI / D_max`, D_max being the brightest light in the
	// scene (VctLighting::update's autoMultiplier), precisely so an 8-bit volume
	// can hold them. The cone-traced diffuse undoes that on the way out --
	// `light.xyz * blendWeight` with blendWeight carrying
	// `vctProbeParams.multiplier` = D_max / PI (Vct_piece_ps.any) -- and so does
	// the fallback ambient in this very file. THE FIELD'S PATH DID NOT.
	//
	// So the field's diffuse was the right picture times 1 / (D_max / PI): a
	// scene whose brightest lamp was dim got a field PI/D_max times TOO BRIGHT
	// (and clipped, the atlas being UNORM), and a scene with a bright lamp got
	// one too dark. MEASURED on a closed room with one lamp, field-lit mean
	// against cone-lit mean at the same pose (spikes/photon-m2, m2-field.js):
	// lamp intensity 0.03 -> 13.4x, 0.12 -> 4.1x, 0.5 -> 1.6x -- i.e. the ratio
	// tracked 1/D_max, capped by the atlas ceiling, exactly as the algebra says.
	// This is also the whole of finding F-C (ledger §659): under 0075 the CONES
	// answered one more bounce pass with +0.15 and the FIELD with +0.03, because
	// at that scene's brightness the field's probes were pinned at the UNORM
	// ceiling (measured: 253-255 of 255) and could not answer anything. The
	// suites never saw it because their lamps are intensity ~1, where the factor
	// is ~1.
	//
	// It belongs HERE and not in the Gen job: the atlas is UNORM, so the
	// normalised form is the only one it can hold without clipping, and this is
	// the same place and the same constant the cone path applies.
	// GUARDED (the lead, from the Fable read at merge): `irradiance_field` is set
	// from the field alone while `vctProbeParams` exists only with a VctLighting
	// bound. Every path today tears the field down before the volume, so the
	// unguarded read was unreachable; the guard makes it impossible.
	@property( vct_num_probes )
	ifdIrradianceScale = float3( passBuf.jahIfd.x * ifdConfidence * ifdWeightFade *
								 passBuf.vctProbeParams.multiplier );
	@else
	ifdIrradianceScale = float3( passBuf.jahIfd.x * ifdConfidence * ifdWeightFade );
	@end
@end

// ---- THE TERMS THAT COMPLEMENT THE FIELD -------------------------------
//
// Inserted AFTER upstream's composite add, in this order, so the accumulation
// order of every term in this file is what it was when the body was a copy.
@piece( custom_ps_ifdCagePost )
@property( vct_num_probes )
	// AND THE FALLBACK (P9). Binding a field sets VctDisableDiffuse
	// (OgreHlmsPbs.cpp:1846-1850), which deletes the ENTIRE voxel-cone diffuse
	// block from the generated shader -- including the one term that block
	// contributes outside the voxel volume, where every cone escapes without
	// hitting anything: ambient x the escape weight, which is ambient x 1
	// (Vct_piece_ps.any:470-477, then x blendFade x multiplier at :480-483,
	// with `blend` hardcoded to 1). That term is exactly what a surface out
	// here had BEFORE the field existed, and fading the field to zero without
	// it would leave the roof flat and BLACK (measured on the base binary with
	// intensity 0: 2/255 against 36/255 with no field at all).
	//
	// It is written as the closed form rather than by re-enabling the cone
	// block, because the block is six cone marches per pixel and this is a
	// lerp: the fallback costs nothing where it is not used (1 - confidence is
	// 0 inside the field) and nothing that scales with anything where it is.
	//
	// NOT DOUBLE-COUNTED, and here is the whole accounting. envColourD receives
	// exactly one diffuse-GI term in every regime: the field's irradiance x
	// confidence, plus our sky-visibility ambient x confidence (below), plus
	// this x (1 - confidence). At confidence 1 this line contributes zero and
	// the frame is what it was; at confidence 0 the two field terms contribute
	// zero and the pixel gets precisely the term VctDisableDiffuse took from
	// it. DoAmbientLighting is not involved in either regime -- it is gated on
	// `vctSpecular.w == 0` and upstream hardcodes that blend to 1 while VCT is
	// bound (AmbientLighting_piece_ps.any:64-72), which is the gap this file's
	// sky-visibility term exists to fill in the first place.
	float ifdFallbackAmbient = 1.0 - ifdConfidence;
@property( vct_disable_diffuse )
	if( ifdFallbackAmbient > 0.0 )
	{
	@property( vct_ambient_hemisphere )
		float3 ifdAmbientOutside = lerp( passBuf.vctProbeParams.ambientLowerHemi.xyz,
										 passBuf.vctProbeParams.ambientUpperHemi.xyz, ambientWD );
	@else
		float3 ifdAmbientOutside = passBuf.vctProbeParams.ambientUpperHemi.xyz;
	@end
		pixelData.envColourD.xyz += ifdAmbientOutside * ( passBuf.vctProbeParams.blendFade *
														 passBuf.vctProbeParams.multiplier *
														 ifdFallbackAmbient );
	}
@else
	// ...AND UNDER A CASCADE CHAIN THE FALLBACK IS THE CHAIN'S OWN BOUNCE, not
	// its ambient (PHOTON_SPEC §13 G3, option (a) as decided).
	//
	// The paragraph above is the single-volume case, where the field covers the
	// whole lit box and the only thing outside its confidence is the world
	// beyond the volume -- for which an unoccluded ambient IS the answer the
	// pixel had before the field existed. A chain is a different geometry: the
	// field rides cascade 0 and the cascades around it hold real, voxelised,
	// light-injected radiance out to the outermost box. Handing the ring a flat
	// ambient there would throw away exactly what the outer cascades were built
	// to compute.
	//
	// So the cone-traced diffuse is switched back on for a chain (the engine's
	// Hlms listener re-opens the gate HlmsPbs closes for any bound field -- see
	// FogHlmsListener::propertiesMergedPreGenerationStep) and the two terms are
	// blended by the field's confidence: the field inside cascade 0, the cones
	// outside it, never both. That is the same hand-off the sky-visibility term
	// above already makes, and it keeps the accounting this file's header
	// states -- envColourD receives exactly ONE diffuse-GI term in every regime.
	//
	// `vctDiffuse` is the cone block's result, and it is already ambient-aware:
	// the escape weight it accumulates (light.w) multiplies the hemisphere pair
	// inside it, so the ring gets precisely the picture it had before any field
	// existed, weighted out as the pixel enters the field's reach.
	pixelData.envColourD.xyz += vctDiffuse.xyz * ifdFallbackAmbient;
@end
@end

@property( ambient_sh && !ambient_sh_monochrome && vct_num_probes )
	// AND THE AMBIENT THE VOLUME SWALLOWED, times the sky visibility above.
	// envColourD ONLY: the VCT specular path still adds its own ambient to
	// envColourS (Vct_piece_ps.any's specular branch), and touching that would
	// count it twice. The world-space normal transform is the one
	// DoAmbientLighting uses (AmbientLighting_piece_ps.any:66-67) — the
	// left-handed cubemap frame with X flipped — because irradianceSH's
	// coefficients are stored in that frame.
	if( passBuf.jahIfd.y > 0.0 && sumIfdWeight > 0.0 )
	{
		float3 ifdWsNormal = mul( passBuf.invViewMatCubemap, pixelData.normal );
		ifdWsNormal.x = -ifdWsNormal.x;
		// x ifdConfidence (P9): this term is the field's own answer about how
		// much sky the pixel can see, so it fades out with the field and the
		// VCT ambient fallback above takes over. Inside the field the factor is
		// exactly 1.
		pixelData.envColourD.xyz += irradianceSH( ifdWsNormal PASSBUF_ARG ) *
									( passBuf.jahIfd.y * ifdSkyVis * ifdConfidence *
									  ifdWeightFade / sumIfdWeight );
	}
@end
@end

@end
